"""Blocks that give the core its floating-point side (RV32 F and D): decode of the FP instructions (including the compressed loads and stores),
the 64-bit load/store split into two word accesses, the serialising hazard, the FP register file and its write-back, the sequencer around the
curated FPU, and the FP control and status registers (fflags, frm, mstatus.FS). Generated by BARG against hand-written golden models
(golden/<name>.py), never shown to the generator. How they join the core: demos/linux-soc/build_core.py --fp."""
from __future__ import annotations

import sys
from pathlib import Path

sys.path.insert(0, str(Path(__file__).parent.parent / "gpu-soc"))
from build_gpu_soc import block, i, o, port  # noqa: E402

W = 32
CLK = port("p_clk", "clk", "in", 1, "clock")
RST = port("p_rst", "rst", "in", 1, "reset")

RVC_FP = block("b_rvc_fp", "rvc_fp", """
    Combinational. inst (32 bits) is the instruction text from the fetch aligner (a compressed instruction is in its low 16 bits) and word_in
    (32 bits) is the 32-bit instruction the integer expander made of it. word_out is word_in, except for eight compressed floating-point
    loads and stores, for which it is the equivalent 32-bit instruction. They are recognised by bits 1:0 and bits 15:13 of inst: bits 1:0 = 00
    with bits 15:13 = 001 (C.FLD), 011 (C.FLW), 101 (C.FSD) or 111 (C.FSW); bits 1:0 = 10 with bits 15:13 = 001 (C.FLDSP), 011 (C.FLWSP), 101
    (C.FSDSP) or 111 (C.FSWSP). Every other value of bits 1:0 and 15:13 (so also every 32-bit instruction, whose bits 1:0 are 11) gives
    word_in. Let rp1 be 8 plus bits 9:7 of inst and rp2 be 8 plus bits 4:2 of inst (5-bit register numbers), rd be bits 11:7 of inst and
    rs2 be bits 6:2 of inst. For the loads (word_out has opcode 0000111): C.FLD and C.FLW: bits 31:20 of word_out are the zero-extended
    offset uimm, bits 19:15 are rp1, bits 11:7 are rp2; C.FLDSP and C.FLWSP: bits 31:20 are the zero-extended offset uimm, bits 19:15 are 2
    (the stack pointer, 00010), bits 11:7 are rd. Bits 14:12 of word_out (the funct3) are 011 for C.FLD and C.FLDSP and 010 for C.FLW and
    C.FLWSP. For the stores (word_out has opcode 0100111): bits 14:12 are 011 for C.FSD and C.FSDSP and 010 for C.FSW and C.FSWSP; bits
    31:25 are uimm[11:5] and bits 11:7 are uimm[4:0] (the offset split as in an S-type instruction); bits 19:15 are rp1 and bits 24:20 are
    rp2 for C.FSD and C.FSW; bits 19:15 are 2 (00010) and bits 24:20 are rs2 for C.FSDSP and C.FSWSP. The offset uimm: C.FLD and C.FSD:
    uimm[7:6] is inst[6:5], uimm[5:3] is inst[12:10], every other bit 0. C.FLW and C.FSW: uimm[6] is inst[5], uimm[5:3] is inst[12:10],
    uimm[2] is inst[6], every other bit 0. C.FLDSP: uimm[5] is inst[12], uimm[4:3] is inst[6:5], uimm[8:6] is inst[4:2], every other bit 0.
    C.FLWSP: uimm[5] is inst[12], uimm[4:2] is inst[6:4], uimm[7:6] is inst[3:2], every other bit 0. C.FSDSP: uimm[5:3] is inst[12:10],
    uimm[8:6] is inst[9:7], every other bit 0. C.FSWSP: uimm[5:2] is inst[12:9], uimm[7:6] is inst[8:7], every other bit 0. No clock, no reset,
    no state.
""", [i("inst", W, "data"), i("word_in", W, "data"), o("word_out", W, "data")])

FP_DEC = block("b_fp_dec", "fp_dec", """
    Combinational, in the decode stage. inst (32 bits) is the instruction word, fs (2 bits) the floating-point unit state (0 means off) and frm
    (3 bits) the dynamic rounding mode. Fields of inst: opcode is bits 6:0, f3 is bits 14:12, fmt is bits 26:25, f5 is bits 31:27, rs2f is
    bits 24:20. rm_ok is 1 when f3 is 0, 1, 2, 3 or 4, or when f3 is 7 and frm is 0, 1, 2, 3 or 4 (f3 equal to 5 or 6 and the rest are not ok).
    The word is a FLOATING-POINT INSTRUCTION when it is one of these: (load) opcode 0000111 with f3 010 or 011; (store) opcode 0100111 with f3
    010 or 011; (fused) opcode 1000011, 1000111, 1001011 or 1001111, with fmt 00 or 01 and rm_ok; (arithmetic) opcode 1010011 with fmt 00 or 01
    and, by f5: 00000, 00001, 00010 or 00011 with rm_ok; 01011 with rs2f 00000 and rm_ok; 00100 with f3 000, 001 or 010; 00101 with f3 000 or
    001; 01000 with rm_ok and either (fmt 00 and rs2f 00001) or (fmt 01 and rs2f 00000); 10100 with f3 000, 001 or 010; 11000 with rs2f 00000
    or 00001 and rm_ok; 11010 with rs2f 00000 or 00001 and rm_ok; 11100 with rs2f 00000 and (f3 001, or f3 000 and fmt 00); 11110 with rs2f
    00000, f3 000 and fmt 00. is_fp is 1 when the word is a floating-point instruction and fs is not 0. is_dw is 1 when is_fp is 1 and opcode is
    0000111 or 0100111 and f3 is 011 (a double-precision load or store). dec_inst (32 bits) is inst when is_fp is 0, and otherwise an integer
    instruction with the same register fields, built so that the integer decoder treats it as a harmless instruction: for a load, the word with
    bits 31:15 of inst, funct3 010, rd 00000 and opcode 0000011; for a store, the word with bits 31:7 of inst, funct3 010 in bits 14:12 and
    opcode 0100011 (bits 31:15 and 11:7 are those of inst); for a fused instruction and for an arithmetic instruction whose f5 is not 10100, 11000
    or 11100: bits 31:25 are 0000000, bits 24:20 are those of inst, bits 19:15 are those of inst, funct3 000, rd 00000 and opcode 0110011; for an
    arithmetic instruction whose f5 is 10100, 11000 or 11100: the same, but with rd being bits 11:7 of inst. No clock, no reset, no state.
""", [i("inst", W, "data"), i("fs", 2, "control"), i("frm", 3, "control"), o("is_fp", 1, "control"), o("is_dw", 1, "control"),
      o("dec_inst", W, "data")])

FP_DWSEQ = block("b_fp_dwseq", "fp_dwseq", """
    Splits a double-precision load or store into two word accesses, in the decode stage. is_dw is 1 when the instruction in decode is a
    double-precision load or store, valid is 1 when it is a real instruction, xcpt is 1 when it already carries an exception; stall is 1 when the
    execute stage holds (decode cannot hand its instruction over) and flush is 1 when the execute stage takes a bubble this cycle. dw is is_dw AND
    valid AND NOT xcpt. Held state, 0 at reset: half2 (1 bit). Outputs, from the state and inputs, before the edge: half2 as held; hold is dw AND NOT
    half2 (the instruction stays in decode for a second pass); dwsel (2 bits) is 00 when dw is 0, 01 when dw is 1 and half2 is 0, and 10 when dw is
    1 and half2 is 1; imm_out (32 bits) is imm_in plus 4 (wrapping) when dw is 1 and half2 is 1, and imm_in otherwise. accept is NOT stall AND NOT
    flush. On each rising clock edge, after reset (reset sets half2 to 0): when flush is 1, half2 becomes 0; otherwise when dw is 1 and accept is 1,
    half2 becomes NOT half2; otherwise when dw is 0, half2 becomes 0; otherwise half2 keeps its value.
""", [CLK, RST, i("is_dw", 1, "control"), i("valid", 1, "control"), i("xcpt", 1, "control"), i("stall", 1, "control"), i("flush", 1, "control"),
      i("imm_in", W, "data"), o("half2", 1, "control"), o("hold", 1, "control"), o("dwsel", 2, "control"), o("imm_out", W, "data")],
    reset="sync", latency=1)

FP_HAZARD = block("b_fp_hazard", "fp_hazard", """
    Combinational. Makes a floating-point instruction in decode wait while an older floating-point instruction that writes a floating-point register
    is in the execute or memory stage, and merges the extra hold of a double-word access into the hazard unit's outputs. stall and flush_id_ex are the
    hazard unit's outputs, md_stall is 1 while the execute stage holds its instruction, dw_hold is 1 while a double-word instruction stays in decode
    for its second pass, id_valid is 1 when decode holds a real instruction, id_is_fp is 1 when that instruction is a floating-point one, half2 is 1
    in the second pass of a double-word instruction. A word is a WRITER when its opcode (bits 6:0) is 0000111, 1000011, 1000111, 1001011, 1001111
    or 1010011. ex_wr is ex_valid AND ex_inst (32 bits) is a writer; mem_wr is mem_valid AND mem_inst (32 bits) is a writer. wait is id_valid AND
    id_is_fp AND NOT half2 AND (ex_wr OR mem_wr). stall_out is stall OR dw_hold OR wait. flush_out is flush_id_ex OR (wait AND NOT md_stall). No
    clock, no reset, no state.
""", [i("stall", 1, "control"), i("flush_id_ex", 1, "control"), i("md_stall", 1, "control"), i("dw_hold", 1, "control"), i("id_valid", 1, "control"),
      i("id_is_fp", 1, "control"), i("half2", 1, "control"), i("ex_valid", 1, "control"), i("ex_inst", W, "data"), i("mem_valid", 1, "control"),
      i("mem_inst", W, "data"), o("stall_out", 1, "control"), o("flush_out", 1, "control")])

FP_PIPE = block("b_fp_pipe", "fp_pipe", """
    The floating-point side of the pipeline registers: it carries, with an instruction, its half-select dwsel (2 bits) from decode to execute, and
    the floating-point result of execute (fres 64 bits, flags 5 bits, wrf 1 bit) on through the memory stage to write-back, along with dwsel. Three
    register stages, all 0 after reset. Decode to execute (dwsel_ex): on each rising clock edge, when rst is 1 or flush_ex is 1 it becomes 0; otherwise
    when stall_ex is 1 it keeps its value; otherwise it becomes dwsel_id. Execute to memory (dwsel_m, fres_m, flags_m, wrf_m): when rst is 1 or
    flush_mem is 1 they become 0; otherwise they become dwsel_ex, fres_ex, flags_ex and wrf_ex. Memory to write-back (dwsel_wb, fres_wb, flags_wb,
    wrf_wb): when rst is 1 or flush_wb is 1 they become 0; otherwise they become dwsel_m, fres_m, flags_m and wrf_m (the values held before the
    edge). Every output is the held value of the register of that name, with no combinational path from an input to an output.
""", [CLK, RST, i("stall_ex", 1, "control"), i("flush_ex", 1, "control"), i("flush_mem", 1, "control"), i("flush_wb", 1, "control"),
      i("dwsel_id", 2, "control"), i("fres_ex", 64, "data"), i("flags_ex", 5, "control"), i("wrf_ex", 1, "control"),
      o("dwsel_ex", 2, "control"), o("dwsel_wb", 2, "control"), o("fres_wb", 64, "data"), o("flags_wb", 5, "control"), o("wrf_wb", 1, "control")],
    reset="sync", latency=1)

FP_EXDEC = block("b_fp_exdec", "fp_exdec", """
    Combinational, in the execute stage. inst (32 bits) is the instruction word, valid is 1 for a real instruction, xcpt is 1 when it carries an
    exception, frm (3 bits) is the dynamic rounding mode. Fields: opcode is bits 6:0, f3 bits 14:12, fmt bits 26:25, f5 bits 31:27, rs2f bits
    24:20. active is valid AND NOT xcpt. is_fpu is active AND opcode is 1000011, 1000111, 1001011, 1001111 or 1010011. is_fst is active AND opcode
    is 0100111 (a floating-point store). int_dest is is_fpu AND opcode is 1010011 AND f5 is 10100, 11000 or 11100 (the result goes to an integer
    register). ra1 is bits 19:15 of inst, ra2 is bits 24:20 and ra3 is bits 31:27 (always). op (5 bits): for opcode 1000011 it is 5, for 1000111
    it is 6, for 1001011 it is 7, for 1001111 it is 8; for opcode 1010011, by f5: 00000 gives 0, 00001 gives 1, 00010 gives 2, 00011 gives 3, 01011
    gives 4, 00100 gives 9 plus f3, 00101 gives 12 plus f3, 01000 gives 22 when fmt is 00 and 23 otherwise, 10100 gives 14 when f3 is 010, 15 when
    f3 is 001 and 16 otherwise, 11000 gives 18 plus bit 0 of rs2f, 11010 gives 20 plus bit 0 of rs2f, 11100 gives 17 when f3 is 001 and 24
    otherwise, 11110 gives 25; every other word gives 0. dbl is bit 0 of fmt (bit 25 of inst), except that it is 0 when opcode is 1010011 and f5 is
    01000. rm (3 bits): when the word is a fused instruction (opcode 1000011, 1000111, 1001011 or 1001111) or has opcode 1010011 with f5 equal to
    00000, 00001, 00010, 00011, 01011, 01000, 11000 or 11010, rm is frm when f3 is 111 and f3 otherwise; for every other word rm is 000. No
    clock, no reset, no state.
""", [i("inst", W, "data"), i("valid", 1, "control"), i("xcpt", 1, "control"), i("frm", 3, "control"), o("is_fpu", 1, "control"),
      o("is_fst", 1, "control"), o("int_dest", 1, "control"), o("op", 5, "control"), o("dbl", 1, "control"), o("rm", 3, "control"),
      o("ra1", 5, "addr"), o("ra2", 5, "addr"), o("ra3", 5, "addr")])

FP_SEQ = block("b_fp_seq", "fp_seq", """
    Sequencer between the execute stage and the floating-point unit. is_fpu is 1 while the instruction in the execute stage needs the unit. The unit
    pulses done (for one cycle) when it has a result, with fres_in (64 bits), ires_in (32 bits), flags_in (5 bits) and wrf_in (1 bit) valid in that
    cycle. Held state, all 0 at reset: st (2 bits: 0 idle, 1 running, 2 finished) and the latched result fres (64 bits), ires (32 bits), flags (5 bits)
    and wrf (1 bit). Outputs, from the state and inputs, before the edge: start is is_fpu AND st is 0; busy is is_fpu AND st is not 2; fres, ires,
    flags and wrf are the latched values. On each rising clock edge, after reset (rst sets everything to 0): when st is 0 and is_fpu is 1, st becomes
    1; when st is 1 and done is 1, the latched values become fres_in, ires_in, flags_in and wrf_in and st becomes 2; when st is 2, st becomes 0;
    otherwise nothing changes. (A finished result stays latched until the next result is latched.)
""", [CLK, RST, i("is_fpu", 1, "control"), i("done", 1, "control"), i("fres_in", 64, "data"), i("ires_in", W, "data"), i("flags_in", 5, "control"),
      i("wrf_in", 1, "control"), o("start", 1, "control"), o("busy", 1, "control"), o("fres", 64, "data"), o("ires", W, "data"),
      o("flags", 5, "control"), o("wrf", 1, "control")], reset="sync", latency=1)

FP_XCPT = block("b_fp_xcpt", "fp_xcpt", """
    Combinational, in the execute stage: the alignment trap of a double-precision access, merged into the execute stage's exception. xcpt_in,
    cause_in (4 bits), tval_in (32 bits) and memwrite_in are the exception unit's outputs, is_store is 1 when the instruction is a store, dwsel (2
    bits) is 01 in the first pass of a double-word access, 10 in the second and 00 otherwise, addr (32 bits) is the access's address. bad is NOT
    xcpt_in AND dwsel is 01 AND bits 2:0 of addr are not 000. xcpt is xcpt_in OR bad. cause is, when bad is 1, 6 when is_store is 1 and 4
    otherwise, and cause_in when bad is 0. tval is addr when bad is 1 and tval_in otherwise. memwrite_out is memwrite_in AND NOT bad. No clock, no
    reset, no state.
""", [i("xcpt_in", 1, "control"), i("cause_in", 4, "control"), i("tval_in", W, "data"), i("memwrite_in", 1, "control"), i("is_store", 1, "control"),
      i("dwsel", 2, "control"), i("addr", W, "addr"), o("xcpt", 1, "control"), o("cause", 4, "control"), o("tval", W, "data"),
      o("memwrite_out", 1, "control")])

FREGFILE = block("b_fregfile", "fregfile", """
    Floating-point register file: 32 registers of 64 bits (register 0 is an ordinary register). Held state: the 32 registers, which are not
    cleared by reset (their contents before the first write are not defined). Three read ports: rdata1, rdata2 and rdata3 (64 bits each) are the
    contents, before the clock edge, of the registers numbered ra1, ra2 and ra3 (5 bits each); a read does not see a write that happens at the
    coming edge. Two write enables with one address: on each rising clock edge, when we_lo is 1 bits 31:0 of the register numbered waddr become
    wdata_lo (32 bits), and when we_hi is 1 bits 63:32 of that register become wdata_hi (32 bits); the two halves are independent.
""", [CLK, i("we_lo", 1, "control"), i("we_hi", 1, "control"), i("waddr", 5, "addr"), i("wdata_lo", W, "data"), i("wdata_hi", W, "data"),
      i("ra1", 5, "addr"), i("ra2", 5, "addr"), i("ra3", 5, "addr"), o("rdata1", 64, "data"), o("rdata2", 64, "data"), o("rdata3", 64, "data")],
    latency=1)

FP_STMUX = block("b_fp_stmux", "fp_stmux", """
    Combinational. The data of a store: y (32 bits) is from_core when is_fst is 0. When is_fst is 1, y is bits 63:32 of fdata (64 bits) when dwsel (2
    bits) is 10 and bits 31:0 of fdata otherwise. No clock, no reset, no state.
""", [i("from_core", W, "data"), i("fdata", 64, "data"), i("is_fst", 1, "control"), i("dwsel", 2, "control"), o("y", W, "data")])

FP_WB = block("b_fp_wb", "fp_wb", """
    Combinational, in the write-back stage: what the instruction there writes into the floating-point register file and the status. inst (32 bits)
    is the instruction, commit is 1 when it retires (it is real and raises no exception), dwsel (2 bits) is 00, 01 or 10 (whole register, first word of
    a double-word access, second word), mem_rdata (32 bits) is the value its load read, fres (64 bits), flags (5 bits) and wrf (1 bit) are the result
    of the floating-point unit. opcode is bits 6:0 of inst. is_load is commit AND opcode is 0000111. is_op is commit AND opcode is 1000011, 1000111,
    1001011, 1001111 or 1010011. waddr is bits 11:7 of inst. we_lo is 1 when is_load is 1 and dwsel is not 10, or when is_op is 1 and wrf is 1. we_hi
    is 1 when is_load is 1 and dwsel is not 01, or when is_op is 1 and wrf is 1. wdata_lo is mem_rdata for a load and bits 31:0 of fres otherwise.
    wdata_hi is, for a load, mem_rdata when dwsel is 10 and FFFFFFFF (hexadecimal) otherwise, and bits 63:32 of fres when it is not a load.
    flags_set (5 bits) is flags when is_op is 1 and 0 otherwise. dirty_set is 1 when is_load is 1, or when is_op is 1 and (wrf is 1 or flags is not
    0). No clock, no reset, no state.
""", [i("inst", W, "data"), i("commit", 1, "control"), i("dwsel", 2, "control"), i("mem_rdata", W, "data"), i("fres", 64, "data"),
      i("flags", 5, "control"), i("wrf", 1, "control"), o("we_lo", 1, "control"), o("we_hi", 1, "control"), o("waddr", 5, "addr"),
      o("wdata_lo", W, "data"), o("wdata_hi", W, "data"), o("flags_set", 5, "control"), o("dirty_set", 1, "control")])

FP_CSR = block("b_fp_csr", "fp_csr", """
    The floating-point control and status registers and the FS field of mstatus, with the read and legality patches for them. Held state, 0 at reset:
    fflags (5 bits), frm (3 bits) and fs (2 bits; 0 off, 1 initial, 2 clean, 3 dirty). inst (32 bits) is the instruction in write-back; addr is bits
    31:20 of inst (the CSR address); csrop (3 bits) is its CSR operation: it accesses a CSR when csrop is 001 (read only), 010, 011 or 100 (binary). csr_we is 1 when a
    CSR write takes effect now; nval (32 bits) is the value written. flags_set (5 bits) and dirty_set come from a retiring floating-point instruction.
    Outputs from the state and inputs, before the edge: fs, frm as held. rdata_in (32 bits) is the CSR read value of the rest of the core and
    illegal_in the rest of the core's illegal-CSR flag. rdata_out is: for addr 001 (hexadecimal) the 5-bit fflags zero-extended; for addr 002 frm
    zero-extended; for addr 003 frm in bits 7:5 and fflags in bits 4:0, zero elsewhere; for addr 300 or 100 rdata_in with bits 14:13 set to fs and
    bit 31 set to 1 when fs is 3 (the other bits of rdata_in kept); for addr 301 rdata_in with bits 3 and 5 set to 1; for every other addr rdata_in.
    illegal_out is, when csrop accesses a CSR and addr is 001, 002 or 003, 1 when fs is 0 and 0 otherwise; in every other case it is illegal_in.
    On each rising clock edge, after reset: when csr_we is 1: for addr 001 fflags becomes bits 4:0 of nval and fs becomes 3; for addr 002 frm becomes
    bits 2:0 of nval and fs becomes 3; for addr 003 frm becomes bits 7:5 of nval, fflags becomes bits 4:0 of nval and fs becomes 3; for addr 300 or
    100 fs becomes bits 14:13 of nval; for every other addr nothing changes. When csr_we is 0: fflags becomes fflags OR flags_set, and when dirty_set
    is 1 fs becomes 3.
""", [CLK, RST, i("inst", W, "data"), i("csrop", 3, "control"), i("csr_we", 1, "control"), i("nval", W, "data"), i("flags_set", 5, "control"),
      i("dirty_set", 1, "control"), i("rdata_in", W, "data"), i("illegal_in", 1, "control"), o("fs", 2, "control"), o("frm", 3, "control"),
      o("rdata_out", W, "data"), o("illegal_out", 1, "control")], reset="sync", latency=1)

FP_OR2 = block("b_fp_or2", "fp_or2", """
    Combinational. y is a OR b. No clock, no reset, no state.
""", [i("a", 1, "control"), i("b", 1, "control"), o("y", 1, "control")])

FP_RESMUX = block("b_fp_resmux", "fp_resmux", """
    Combinational. The execute stage's result: y (32 bits) is from_fp when sel is 1 and from_alu when sel is 0. No clock, no reset, no state.
""", [i("from_alu", W, "data"), i("from_fp", W, "data"), i("sel", 1, "control"), o("y", W, "data")])

BLOCKS = [FP_RESMUX, RVC_FP, FP_DEC, FP_DWSEQ, FP_HAZARD, FP_PIPE, FP_EXDEC, FP_SEQ, FP_XCPT, FREGFILE, FP_STMUX, FP_WB, FP_CSR, FP_OR2]
